<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/10th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chenqiao Mutiny</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/960">960 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Chen Qiao Mutiny</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chenqiao Mutiny was the coup of 960 CE in which troops of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-zhou">Later Zhou</a> commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-song">Zhao Kuangyin</a> draped him in the imperial yellow robe and proclaimed him emperor. Zhao took the reign name Taizu. The event marked the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chenqiao-mutiny.jpg" alt="Chenqiao Mutiny" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry Charles Seppings Wright · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhao_Kuangyin_is_proclaimed_emperor_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a>'s rebellion broke the Tang court's hold on the provinces, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was ruled by the Five Dynasties, each supplanting the last by usurpation or war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-zhou">Later Zhou</a>, founded in 951, was the last of them. Its emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guo-wei">Guo Wei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chai-rong">Chai Rong</a> curbed the regional military governors, centralised the army and won territory from the Later Shu, the Southern Tang and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-song">Zhao Kuangyin</a>, son of the general Zhao Hongyin, helped Guo Wei seize power, distinguished himself in Chai Rong's campaigns and became inspector-general of the Palace Command, which let him build up his troops' personal loyalty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chai-rong">Chai Rong</a> died in 959 and was succeeded by his five-year-old son, Guo Zongxun. When a joint invasion by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> and the Northern Han was reported early in 960, the court sent Zhao against it despite warnings about his influence. He crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> on 1 February and camped at Chenqiao, a village north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>.</p><p>That night his officers agreed to make him emperor, with the support of his younger brother Zhao Kuangyi and his adviser Zhao Pu. According to the History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, at dawn on 2 February they burst into his room and draped him in a yellow imperial robe, and he agreed to lead them back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> only if they swore loyalty and did not loot the capital.</p><p>Traditional accounts present Zhao as an unwilling victim of circumstance, and later works added portents such as a second sun seen by the astrologer Miao Xun. Most modern historians, among them Deng Guangming, Chen Dengyuan and Wang Boqin, hold that Zhao, his brother and key advisers engineered the mutiny; the yellow robe was probably a later addition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Han Tong, the only serious opponent in the capital, was killed, and the child emperor abdicated and was exiled. The handover was so peaceful that the street markets kept trading. Revolts by Li Yun and Li Chongjin were each defeated in under two months. In 971, according to the Sushui Jiwen, Zhao persuaded his top generals to give up their commands in exchange for marriages into the royal family. His brother completed the reunification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> with the conquest of the Northern Han in 979.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chenqiao mutiny” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Song Dynasty was founded in 960 CE by Zhao Kuangyin, a military commander of the Later Zhou who seized power in the <b>Chenqiao Mutiny</b>, when his troops draped him in the imperial yellow robe and proclaimed him emperor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The founder, Zhao Kuangyin, was a general whose troops proclaimed him emperor in the <b>Chenqiao Mutiny</b> of 960; as Emperor Taizu he placed the army under civil officials.</blockquote><a href="/societies/song-china">Social Hierarchy of Song China</a></li><li><blockquote>In 960 the palace army commander Zhao Kuangyin was proclaimed emperor by his troops at <b>Chenqiao</b> and founded the Song Dynasty, which built on Later Zhou's army and policies.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-zhou">Later Zhou</a></li></ul></section>
